<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Columba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">521–597</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Columba of Iona, Colm Cille, Columcille</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Columba, or Colm Cille, was an Irish monk of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-conaill">Cenél Conaill</a>, a branch of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, who founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> off western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> around 563. From Iona he and his monks spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, and according to his biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> he ordained King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a>. Iona became a leading centre of learning and of the Irish church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columba.jpg" alt="Columba" width="300" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. R. Skelton (Joseph Ratcliffe Skelton; 1865–1927) (illustrator), erroneously credited as John R. Skelton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columba_at_Bridei%27s_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 563 the Irish monk <b>Columba</b> founded a monastery on the island of Iona, and according to Adomnán’s Life of Columba his community kept close ties with the kings of Dál Riata.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 900, under Donald II and Constantine II, the kingdom was called Alba, with its royal centre at Scone and relics of <b>Columba</b> at Dunkeld.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leading king, Aedan mac Gabrain, was ordained by <b>Columba of Iona</b> according to Adomnan, and campaigned widely in northern Britain before his defeat by Aethelfrith of Bernicia at Degsastan in 603.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-ngabrain">Cenel nGabrain</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhydderch Hael, a king of the late 6th century, appears in the lives of <b>Columba</b> and Kentigern, the patron saint of Glasgow.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Columba of Iona</b> belonged to this kindred, which also shared the high kingship in the early centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-chonaill">Kingdom of Tir Chonaill</a></li></ul></section>
